##Lesson 8 — Windows Network Connection Investigation

#Objective

Investigate a controlled network connection using Sysmon Event ID 3 and correlate it with Sysmon Event ID 1 to identify the responsible process and its parent process.

#Evidence Collected

#Sysmon Event ID 1 — Process Creation

- UTC timestamp: 2026-10-22 02:34:19
- Image: "C:\Windows\System32\WindowsPowerShell\v1.0\powershell.exe"
- Process ID: "2544"
- Parent image: "C:\Windows\explorer.exe"
- Parent Process ID: "512"
- Command line: ""C:\Windows\System32\WindowsPowerShell\v1.0\powershell.exe""
- ProcessGuid: "{890b2ad7-762b-6ad9-0f02-000000002900}"

![ProcessCreation](../lesson08_event1.png)
![Process Creation](../lesson08_event1.png)

##Sysmon Event ID 3 — Network Connection

- UTC timestamp: 2026-10-22 02:34:27
- Image: "C:\Windows\System32\WindowsPowerShell\v1.0\powershell.exe"
- Process ID: "2544"
- Source IP: "10.0.2.15"
- Destination IP: "1.1.1.1"
- Destination port: "443"
- Protocol: TCP
- ProcessGuid: "{890b2ad7-762b-6ad9-0f02-000000002900}"

![NetworkConnection](../lesson08_event3.png)
![Network Connection](../lesson08_event3.png)


##Analysis

The ProcessGuid and Process ID matched across Event ID 1 and Event ID 3, correlating the network connection with the PowerShell process created at 02:34:19 UTC.

The network connection was recorded at 02:34:27 UTC, eight seconds after process creation. The parent image was Windows Explorer.

The connection was generated during a controlled lab exercise using "Test-NetConnection". This context is consistent with the observed network activity.

##Conclusion

The evidence establishes a correlation between the PowerShell process and the recorded TCP connection to "1.1.1.1" on port 443.

The activity is consistent with the controlled lab test. The two events do not independently establish malicious activity or prove that the endpoint is compromised.

##Recommendations

1. Preserve the relevant Event ID 1 and Event ID 3 records.
2. Use ProcessGuid to correlate additional Sysmon events associated with the same process.
3. Review command-line and surrounding process activity when investigating unfamiliar PowerShell network connections.
4. Correlate network telemetry with DNS, endpoint, and other relevant security logs when investigating unexplained activity.
5. Avoid classifying activity as malicious without sufficient supporting evidence.
